    "agent_messages": [
      "The kernel already handles eligibility, locking, idempotency and audit, so the new endpoint only needs a reject Transition and a route.\nI added `POST /purchases/bulk-reject`. It builds and `go test ./...` passes, but there are no test files in the repo and no database credentials, so none of the reject behaviour has actually been run.\n\nThe only file changed is `internal/candidate/approval.go`:\n- **New Action:** I declared `Reject = action.Transition{Operation: \"bulk-reject.v4\", From: \"submitted\", To: \"rejected\", Field: \"rejectedIds\"}`.\n- **Shared handler:** I pulled the existing approve handler out into a small function that works for either Action. It still turns `action.Rejection` into `protocol.Rejection` at this boundary.\n- **Routes:** `/purchases/bulk-approve` (unchanged behaviour) and the new `/purchases/bulk-reject` are both registered in `protocol.Routes` and served by `protocol.HTTP`. That adapter already does the login, approver-role, body-size, request-body and `Idempotency-Key` checks.\n\nI didn't write any new rule logic. From reading the code, the existing `Executor.Run` and the schema already cover the rest of the spec:\n- **Who and which records:** the caller must be an approver, and targets must be in the caller's tenant, created by someone else and currently `submitted`. If any target fails this, the whole batch gets a 409 and the transaction rolls back.\n- **Idempotency:** results are keyed by operation, tenant, actor and key. A replay of the same set returns the stored response with the replay header set to `true`; the same key with a different set returns 409. The `ActionRequest` row is written in the same transaction as the status change.\n- **Concurrency:** a per-key lock plus row locks on the targets. An update that touches fewer rows than requested returns 409, so a concurrent approve and reject on the same record can't both succeed.\n- **Failures:** any other database error, including a failed `ActionRequest` write, rolls back and returns 503. The same key can then be retried.\n- **Database policy:** the schema's `purchase_decide` policy only allows updating `submitted` rows. That stops rejected records being approved or rejected again, and approved records being rejected.\n- **Identity:** it comes only from the demo `Authorization` token, so client headers can't override it.\n\nI added no tests, because the instructions say tests are protected."
    ],
